#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d2284f-c4ba-571f-91a6-be472f185c0b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Negative"}] :question/id #uuid "27032e03-f6b6-5f66-b514-d3b9c4f79ec0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1352" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{y^4 z^{6}}$ given that $z \\lt 0$." :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{y^4 z^{6}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{y^2 z^3 \\cdot y^2 z^3} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{\\left(y^2 z^3\\right)^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\begin{vmatrix}y^2 z^3 & \\\\[5pt] &= & y^2 & \\cdot & z^3 & \\\\[5pt] &= & y^2 & \\cdot\\left.\\end{vmatrix}z\\right|^3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\mid y^2 \\mid = y^2\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that $z \\lt 0$, and to take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign, so we have\n\n$$\n\\mid z \\mid = -z\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}y^{2} & \\cdot\\left.\\end{vmatrix}z\\right|^{3} = y^{2} \\cdot (-z)^{3} =-y^{2}z^{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b8f0a91-f0de-5907-9c96-eac8a3a0e7cf" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Non-Negative"}] :question/id #uuid "4b7c6339-6e9c-5047-a95f-3daf41db4a8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1353" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{s^2}}$ given that $s>0$." :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{s^2}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{r}{s} \\cdot \\dfrac{r}{s}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{r}{s}\\right)^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\left|\\dfrac{r}{s}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{\\begin{vmatrix}r &}{\\mid s\\end{vmatrix}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $s \\gt 0$, and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\mid s \\mid = s\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\begin{vmatrix}r &}{\\begin{vmatrix}s &} = \\dfrac{\\end{vmatrix}r\\end{vmatrix}}{s}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b8f0a91-f0de-5907-9c96-eac8a3a0e7cf" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Non-Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0cfe5f9-b62f-5b51-ab1d-bce042907edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178516} {:answer/id #uuid "1a73cf8e-00c3-5c0e-a4a4-3dd14653ca57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178517} {:answer/id #uuid "414b8ab5-e58e-5e5f-afd8-5d24e4765f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178518} {:answer/id #uuid "50d7d27e-a8b8-55fb-920e-3989c1684b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178519} {:answer/id #uuid "5af6a186-f70b-53bc-a5e9-a363d3f26134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178520}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9d10b6a6-f98d-52cb-975a-842633722f73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-fiel